{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-330948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}},$ find the Laplace transform of $f(t)=(3t+4)^{2}$ for $s>0.$\n\n$L{f(t)}=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-330948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The linearity property of the Laplace transform states that for functions $f(t)$ and $g(t),$ we have\n\n$$\nL{\\alpha f(t)+\\beta g(t)}=\\alpha \\cdot L{f(t)}+\\beta \\cdot L{g(t)}\n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants.\n\nFirst, we expand:\n\n$$\nf(t)=(3t+4)^{2}=9{t}^{2}+24t+16\n$$\n\nTaking the Laplace transform and applying the linearity properties, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =L{9{t}^{2}+24t+16} \\\\  & =L{9{t}^{2}}+L{24t}+L{16} \\\\  & =9L{{t}^{2}}+24L{t}+16L{1}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe're given that $L{{t}^{n}}=\\frac{n!}{{s}^{n+1}}.$ Therefore, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}} & =\\frac{2}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{t} & =\\frac{1}{{s}^{2}},\\,s>0 \\\\ L{1} & =\\frac{1}{s},\\,s>0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the Laplace transform of $f(t)$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{f(t)} & =9L{{t}^{2}}+24L{t}+16L{1} \\\\  & =9\\cdot \\frac{2}{{s}^{3}}+24\\cdot \\frac{1}{{s}^{2}}+16\\cdot \\frac{1}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{18}{{s}^{3}}+\\frac{24}{{s}^{2}}+\\frac{16}{s} \\\\  & =\\frac{18+24s+16{s}^{2}}{{s}^{3}} \\\\  & =\\frac{2(8{s}^{2}+12s+9)}{{s}^{3}},\\,s>0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-330948" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fc6737efff481bdca94dbf64cdeca031f12322707bd827a273b1cc87496bbb3"} {"question" "q-330948" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-330948" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{18}{s^3}+\\frac{24}{s^2}+\\frac{16}{s}"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-332674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Let $f(t)$ be a piecewise function defined on $[0,\\infty )$ with exponential order $a,$ and consider its Laplace transform \n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)},\\,s>a.\n$$\n\nWhich of the following statements are true?\n\n- If $f(t)$ contains a discontinuity for some value of $t>0,$ we have $F(s)=0$ for all $s>a.$\n- $F(s)$ is infinitely differentiable on $s>a.$\n- The Laplace transform converts a non-smooth $f(t)$ into a smooth function of $s.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-332674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nLet's consider each statement in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Smoothness has no bearing on whether the Laplace transform vanishes.\nA function of exponential order always has a well-defined Laplace transform for $s>a$, and there is no reason for that transform to be identically zero.\n\n- Statement II is true. The smoothness theorem states that for $f(t)$ of exponential order, the Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f}(s)\n$$\n\nis infinitely differentiable at every point in its region of convergence, and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}(s).\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. The Laplace transform $F(s)$ of a function $f(t)$ is always infinitely differentiable in its region of convergence, even if $f(t)$ is non-smooth.\n\nTherefore, the answer is \"II and III only.\"" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-332674" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fc6737efff481bdca94dbf64cdeca031f12322707bd827a273b1cc87496bbb3"} {"question" "q-332674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-332674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "I and III only"] ["text" "I only"] ["text" "II and III only"] ["text" "II only"] ["text" "III only"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-332674" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "II and III only"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose that $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for $s>a.$ Then, $F(s)$ is twice differentiable at every point $s>a,$ and we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}.\n$$\n\nA proof of this statement is given below. \n\n$\\text{L1}:\\,$ $F(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$ exists for $s>a.$\n\n$\\text{L2}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L3}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L4}:\\,$ $\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)$\n\n$\\text{L5}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt$\n\n$\\text{L6}:\\,$ ${F}^{″}(s)=L{{t}^{2}\\cdot f(t)}$\n\nSelect the correct options in the following reasoning.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L2}$ follows from line $\\text{L1}$ and\n{{field-1}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and {{field-2}}.\n\n$\\,$ Line $\\text{L4}$ follows from {{field-3}}." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. \n\nThe smoothness property states that, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and $s>a,$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{(n)}(s)=(-1)^{n}\\,L{{t}^{n}f(t)}.\n$$\n\nWe're asked to prove the smoothness property in the case $n=2.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each statement in turn.\n\n- We first consider line $\\text{L2}.$ From line $\\text{L1}$ we know that\n\n$$\nF(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nBy $\\text{the definition of the second derivative with respect to }s$, we get\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}F(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt,\n$$\n\nwhich is exactly the statement in line $\\text{L2}.$\n\n- Next we consider line $\\text{L3}.$ From $\\text{L2}$ we have\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{{d}^{2}}{d{s}^{2}}{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }{e}^{-st}f(t)\\,dt.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is of exponential order, the integral converges, and we can\ndifferentiate under the integral sign:\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))\\,dt.\n$$\n\nTherefore, line $\\text{L3}$ follows from $\\text{L2}$ and $\\text{differentiating under the integral sign}.$\n\n- We now consider line $\\text{L4}.$ The function $f(t)$ does not depend on $s$, so\n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st}f(t))=f(t)\\,\\frac{{∂}^{2}}{∂{s}^{2}}({e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,\\frac{∂}{∂s}(-t{e}^{-st})=f(t)\\,({t}^{2}{e}^{-st})={t}^{2}{e}^{-st}f(t).\n$$\n\nThus line $\\text{L4}$ follows from $\\text{the chain rule}.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "1fc6737efff481bdca94dbf64cdeca031f12322707bd827a273b1cc87496bbb3"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "the definition of the Laplace transform"] ["text" "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the definition of the second derivative with respect to $s$"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "differentiating under the integral sign"] ["text" "integration by parts"] ["text" "the continuity of partial derivatives"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-2" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "differentiating under the integral sign"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/select "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["text" "the chain rule"] ["text" "the linearity of the Laplace transform"] ["text" "the product rule"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-334874" "field" "field-3" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/text "the chain rule"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-340844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Suppose we wish to calculate the Laplace transform $L{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}$ using the smoothness property. Select the correct options in the reasoning below.Let $f(t)={e}^{4t}$ and $F(s)=L{f(t)}.$ By the smoothness property of Laplace transforms, we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nWe have that\n\n$$\n{F}^{″}(s)={{field-2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={{field-3}},\\,s>4.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "file_sha256" "93fe219c3c913a6acc3484ed6a171f29d4cb12ef105138719cc6d8d0c09d379d"} {"question" "q-340844" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "If a function $f(t)$ is piecewise continuous on $[0,\\infty )$ and has exponential order $a,$ then its Laplace transform\n\n$$\nF(s)=L{f(t)}\n$$\n\nexists for every real $s>a,$ and $F$ is infinitely differentiable in this region of convergence. Moreover, for each integer $n\\ge 0$ and every such $s,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{n}f(t)}=(-1)^{n}{F}^{(n)}(s).\n$$\n\nIn particular, for $n=2,$ we have\n\n$$\nL{{t}^{2}f(t)}={F}^{″}(s).\n$$\n\nWe have that $f(t)={e}^{4t}.$ Therefore, for $s>4,$ we have\n\n$$\nF(s)=\\frac{1}{s-4}.\n$$\n\nDifferentiating with respect to $s,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{F}^{′}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(\\frac{1}{s-4}) \\\\  & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}((s-4)^{-1}) \\\\  & =-(s-4)^{-2} \\\\ {F}^{″}(s) & =\\frac{\\text{d}}{\\text{d}s}(-(s-4)^{-2}) \\\\  & =2(s-4)^{-3} \\\\  & =\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, by the smoothness property,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}L{{t}^{2}{e}^{4t}}={F}^{″}(s)=\\frac{2}{(s-4)^{3}},\\,s>4.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14074297/state.json" "fi